WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.107
An outlier is an observation that looks unusual compared with the rest of the data.

00:00:05.107 --> 00:00:11.532
It may be an error, but it may also be a legitimate and important case.

00:00:11.532 --> 00:00:16.772
For example, a very large order could be a misplaced decimal point, a wholesale customer,

00:00:16.772 --> 00:00:19.452
or a real high-value transaction.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:22.472
Those possibilities require different actions.

00:00:22.971 --> 00:00:28.718
This is why I want you to think of outliers as questions rather than automatic deletions.

00:00:28.718 --> 00:00:33.698
A useful workflow is detect, investigate, then decide.

00:00:33.698 --> 00:00:41.618
You may keep the value, correct it using a trusted source, transform the variable, analyze it separately,

00:00:41.618 --> 00:00:44.878
or exclude it when you have a defensible reason.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:49.613
Simply writing “I removed all outliers” is not enough.

00:00:49.613 --> 00:00:57.023
In a professional analysis, you should explain how the unusual values were identified and why the chosen treatment makes

00:00:57.023 --> 00:00:57.881
sense.
